comments[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-26T10:31:20.386Z","harness:opencode","Corpus-gap evidence 2026-07-26 (audit plan pm-t9e3bc): in this extension-loaded dogfood workspace the agent-facing payloads exceed every gated fixture budget — pm --help renders 13,398 bytes vs the 11,688-byte fixture budget (7 loaded extensions contribute 21 command handlers the fixture never sees), and contracts --flags-only measures 117,070 bytes vs the 116,189-byte fixture budget. Both gates are green because the fixture is extension-free and micro-scale; the real workspace an agent actually reads is already over budget on both surfaces. Confirms this item's thesis from the agent side: the largest payloads (extension-loaded help/contracts) are the unmeasured ones."
  "2026-07-28T11:53:10.531Z","harness:codex","Evidence: token-budget manifest v2 records scale tiers plus measured byte/token baselines, separates discovery baselines from answer costs, and dynamically resolves answer ceilings from pm contracts. The reproducible gate covers 23 surfaces across list/get/context/next/activity/deps tree/deps graph/duplicates/events/health/validate/search in TOON and JSON where applicable, exposes headroom deltas, and rejects missing or stale surfaces. Final gate and static quality pass."
notes[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-26T10:54:58.693Z","harness:claude-code","Corpus evidence 2026-07-26: the largest agent-facing payload in the CLI is pm context --explain-ranking at 131,187 bytes TOON / 161,179 JSON for a two-row request on a 280-item workspace, and it is absent from the token-gate corpus. It also scales with workspace size rather than with the request, so measuring it on the seeded micro-workspace understates it by the ratio of workspace sizes rather than by a constant. Owned as a defect by pm-okgxwa; this note records why the corpus must include it regardless of how the defect is fixed."
  "2026-07-26T11:30:03.695Z","harness:claude-code","Corpus coverage recheck 2026-07-26 on the shipped 2026.7.26 build, adding the two payloads that are larger than anything currently in scope.\n\nThe gate's 14 budgets are all help, contracts, list, get, context, next, and search. Measured against the real tracker (2,076 items), the two largest agent-facing command payloads are absent:\n\n  pm graph impact pm-doxj   230,452 B   ~57,613 tokens   1,824 rows, no default bound\n  pm health                 132,584 B   ~33,146 tokens   every check reporting ok\n\nBoth exceed the MCP tools/list handshake (38,496 B) that this item already names as its largest known omission, and both are recurring per-invocation costs rather than a once-per-session fixed cost.\n\nAlso worth folding into the fixture argument: pm context --limit 5 is budgeted at 275 tokens on the seeded fixture and measures 1,524 tokens here, a 5.5x understatement on the same command this item already cites at 1,803 tokens for --limit 10. The ratio is stable across limits, which is the cleanest available demonstration that the fixture scales the ceiling rather than the product.\n\nBoth new payloads have owners for the defect itself — pm-mfy1ux for the unbounded graph impact default, pm-du93sr for pm health --check-only being inert against a 233x cheaper --summary — so this item's remaining share is only that neither surface is in the measured corpus, and that a fix landing on either would have no ratchet holding it."
